+// The W^X protocol wants a page's mark cleared and mmu->changes bumped *after*
+// the store that cleared it has landed: cleared before it, a compile between
+// the two marks the page again, reads the old bytes, sees nothing move, and
+// keeps a block of them that the store then lands under. mem_ptr still clears
+// before (so no writable TLB entry is ever stamped over a marked page), and
+// says so on this thread through mmu_code_cleared_take; the caller makes the
+// store itself and then calls mmu_code_store_landed, which clears and bumps
+// again and drops the page's blocks under asbestos->lock. The lock is what
+// catches a compile already past its check: compiling and inserting happen
+// under it, so its block is in the page's list by the time the drop runs.
+void mmu_code_cleared_reset(void);
+bool mmu_code_cleared_take(void);
+void mmu_code_store_landed(struct mmu *mmu, page_t page);
